{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target commits a category error by conflating the evolutionary origin of a belief with its rational justification. While evolutionary epistemology may explain why scientists *do* prefer corroborated theories (a descriptive claim), the problem explicitly asks if this preference is *defensible* on Popper's terms (a normative claim). Popper's framework demands a logical rationale for acting on one theory over another without invoking induction; answering that 'we are evolved to do so' bypasses the normative requirement entirely. Furthermore, the target's own countercondition admits that if evolutionary success does not correlate with truth-likeness, the argument fails, yet provides no account within Popper's system for why we should assume such a correlation exists without resorting to the very inductive assumptions Popper rejected.","premise":"Popper's problem requires a normative justification for preferring corroborated theories that avoids induction, not merely a descriptive explanation of why the preference exists.","premises_essential":["8b7a268bae2fd1c2286a3080f5f60e5b45a41a555030e0c80f3b13d519a43ce3"],"successor_question":"If the preference for corroborated theories is solely an evolutionary trait with no logical link to truth, how can a Popperian scientist rationally claim that acting on this preference is any more reasonable than acting on a random choice?","target":"8b7a268bae2fd1c2286a3080f5f60e5b45a41a555030e0c80f3b13d519a43ce3"}]}